IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Canada Business Corporations Act (R.S.C. 1985, c. C-44) -- THE SHARED VOCABULARY.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. The text this was encoded from is a plain-text rendering of the Department of Justice
-- Canada consolidation current to 2026-09-30 (last amended 2026-03-26); it is not an official version,
-- and nothing here has been verified against the official text. Contains information licensed under
-- the Open Government Licence - Canada.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS. Only the nouns two or more pieces of the Act will need: the kinds of person and
-- body the Act names, the parties it gives duties to, the offices, the provinces, the kinds of
-- resolution, a tally of votes, a block of shares, and the one way a time limit is written. It holds no
-- rule of the Act: the rules that define the Act's own terms are in cbca-core-definitions.l4,
-- cbca-core-control.l4 and cbca-core-isc.l4, which import this module.
--
-- CONVENTIONS (the same text is in NOTES.md, "Naming conventions"):
--   money      a NUMBER of Canadian dollars, cents as decimals (the Act has dollar amounts only in Part IV
--              and Part XX); a name that holds one ends "in dollars".
--   fraction   a NUMBER from 0 to 1, written with the postfix %: 25% is 0.25, never 25. A name that holds
--              one starts "fraction of".
--   days       a NUMBER of calendar days. A time limit in any unit is a `Time limit` (below).
--   dates      a DATE built with `Date day month year`, or with YMD below, which only this module defines.
--   not covered  a MAYBE with NOTHING, or a named constructor; never FALSE or 0.

§ `The shared vocabulary of the Canada Business Corporations Act`

§§ `Kinds of person and entity -- s 2(1) "person", "entity"`

-- One enumeration serves both definitions, because they overlap: "person" lists an individual,
-- partnership, association, body corporate and personal representative; "entity" lists a body corporate,
-- partnership, trust, joint venture and unincorporated association or organization. The rules that say
-- which constructors are a "person" and which are an "entity" are in cbca-core-definitions.l4.
-- src:31 | body corporate includes a company or other body corporate wherever or however incorporated; (personne morale)
-- src:46 | entity means a body corporate, a partnership, a trust, a joint venture or an unincorporated association or organization; (entité)
-- src:50 | individual means a natural person; (particulier)
-- src:56 | person means an individual, partnership, association, body corporate, or personal representative; (personne)
-- src:57 | personal representative means a person who stands in place of and represents another person including, but not limited to, a trustee, an executor, an administrator, a liquidator of a succession, an administrator of the property of others, a guardian or tutor, a curator, a receiver or sequestrator, an agent or mandatary or an attorney; (représentant personnel)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) body corporate, entity, individual, person, personal representative (src:31 src:46 src:50 src:56-57)
DECLARE `Kind of person or entity` IS ONE OF
    `an individual`
    `a partnership`
    `an association`
    `a body corporate`
    `a personal representative`
    `a trust`
    `a joint venture`
    `an unincorporated association or organization`

§§ `The parties the Act names`

-- The offices and roles that the Act gives powers or duties to. Use these as the PARTY of a regulative
-- rule (DEONTIC Party <the piece's own action type>). The Act defines some (the Director, the Minister,
-- a director, an officer, an incorporator, the auditor); the others (shareholder, security holder,
-- creditor, trustee, receiver, liquidator, proxyholder, insider, offeror, complainant) it names and
-- defines only in the Part where each is used, so those are not quoted here.
-- src:28 | auditor includes a partnership of auditors or an auditor that is incorporated; (vérificateur)
-- src:43 | Director means the Director appointed under section 260; (directeur)
-- src:44 | director means a person occupying the position of director by whatever name called and directors and board of directors includes a single director; (administrateur et conseil d’administration)
-- src:49 | incorporator means a person who signs articles of incorporation; (fondateur)
-- src:53 | Minister means such member of the Queen’s Privy Council for Canada as is designated by the Governor in Council as the Minister for the purposes of this Act; (ministre)
-- src:54 | officer means an individual appointed as an officer under section 121, the chairperson of the board of directors, the president, a vice-president, the secretary, the treasurer, the comptroller, the general counsel, the general manager, a managing director, of a corporation, or any other individual who performs functions for a corporation similar to those normally performed by an individual occupying any of those offices; (dirigeant)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) auditor, Director, director, incorporator, Minister, officer (src:28 src:43-44 src:49 src:53-54)
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the corporation`
    `an incorporator`
    `a director`
    `an officer`
    `a shareholder`
    `a security holder`
    `a creditor`
    `the auditor`
    `a trustee under a trust indenture`
    `a receiver or receiver-manager`
    `a liquidator`
    `a proxyholder`
    `an insider`
    `an offeror`
    `a complainant`
    `the Director`
    `the Minister`
    `the court`
    `any person`

§§ `The offices of a corporation -- s 2(1) "officer"`

-- The offices the definition of "officer" lists, plus the two ends of the definition: an officer
-- appointed under s 121, and any other individual who performs functions similar to those normally
-- performed by the holder of one of the offices. `holds no office and performs no similar function`
-- is the answer that the individual is not an officer. Part XI (insider trading, s 125) has its own
-- definition of "officer" with a similar list; that piece defines its own rule and may reuse this type.
-- src:54 | officer means an individual appointed as an officer under section 121, the chairperson of the board of directors, the president, a vice-president, the secretary, the treasurer, the comptroller, the general counsel, the general manager, a managing director, of a corporation, or any other individual who performs functions for a corporation similar to those normally performed by an individual occupying any of those offices; (dirigeant)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) officer (src:54)
DECLARE Office IS ONE OF
    `an officer appointed under section 121`
    `the chairperson of the board of directors`
    `the president`
    `a vice-president`
    `the secretary`
    `the treasurer`
    `the comptroller`
    `the general counsel`
    `the general manager`
    `a managing director`
    `another individual performing functions similar to those of one of these offices`
    `holds no office and performs no similar function`

§§ `The kinds of articles -- s 2(1) "articles"`

-- src:20 | articles means the original or restated articles of incorporation, articles of amendment, articles of amalgamation, articles of continuance, articles of reorganization, articles of arrangement, articles of dissolution, articles of revival and includes any amendments thereto; (statuts)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) articles (src:20)
DECLARE `Kind of articles` IS ONE OF
    `the original articles of incorporation`
    `restated articles of incorporation`
    `articles of amendment`
    `articles of amalgamation`
    `articles of continuance`
    `articles of reorganization`
    `articles of arrangement`
    `articles of dissolution`
    `articles of revival`

§§ `Provinces and territories -- s 2(1) "court"`

-- The thirteen places the definition of "court" names. Used wherever the Act turns on a province (the
-- court, the registered office, the law of a province).
-- src:34 | court means
-- src:35 | (a) in the Province of Newfoundland and Labrador, the Trial Division of the Supreme Court of the Province,
-- src:36 | (a.1) in the Province of Ontario, the Superior Court of Justice,
-- src:37 | (b) in the Provinces of Nova Scotia, British Columbia and Prince Edward Island, the Supreme Court of the Province,
-- src:38 | (c) in the Provinces of Manitoba, Saskatchewan, Alberta and New Brunswick, the Court of Queen’s Bench for the Province,
-- src:39 | (d) in the Province of Quebec, the Superior Court of the Province, and
-- src:40 | (e) the Supreme Court of Yukon, the Supreme Court of the Northwest Territories and the Nunavut Court of Justice; (tribunal)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) court (src:34-40)
DECLARE `Province or territory` IS ONE OF
    `Newfoundland and Labrador`
    `Prince Edward Island`
    `Nova Scotia`
    `New Brunswick`
    `Quebec`
    `Ontario`
    `Manitoba`
    `Saskatchewan`
    `Alberta`
    `British Columbia`
    `Yukon`
    `Northwest Territories`
    `Nunavut`

§§ `Resolutions and votes -- s 2(1) "ordinary resolution", "special resolution"`

-- src:55 | ordinary resolution means a resolution passed by a majority of the votes cast by the shareholders who voted in respect of that resolution; (résolution ordinaire)
-- src:72 | special resolution means a resolution passed by a majority of not less than two-thirds of the votes cast by the shareholders who voted in respect of that resolution or signed by all the shareholders entitled to vote on that resolution; (résolution spéciale)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) ordinary resolution, special resolution (src:55 src:72)
DECLARE `Kind of resolution` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary resolution`
    `a special resolution`

-- The votes on one resolution. Only votes cast by shareholders who voted count; a shareholder who did
-- not vote adds nothing to either number. Votes are counted, not shareholders: a share with ten votes
-- adds ten.
-- src:55 | ordinary resolution means a resolution passed by a majority of the votes cast by the shareholders who voted in respect of that resolution; (résolution ordinaire)
-- src:72 | special resolution means a resolution passed by a majority of not less than two-thirds of the votes cast by the shareholders who voted in respect of that resolution or signed by all the shareholders entitled to vote on that resolution; (résolution spéciale)
@ref CBCA s 2(1) ordinary resolution, special resolution (src:55 src:72)
DECLARE `Vote tally` HAS
    `votes cast in favour` IS A NUMBER
    `votes cast against` IS A NUMBER
    `signed by all the shareholders entitled to vote on the resolution` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `A block of shares -- s 2.1(3)`

-- Shares, measured the two ways the Act measures them: by the votes they carry and by their fair market
-- value. A holder's block and the corporation's outstanding shares are both blocks, so the same type
-- serves for "a significant number of shares" (s 2.1(3)) and for any other share-fraction test.
-- For the corporation's whole: `votes carried` is the votes attached to ALL its outstanding VOTING
-- shares, and `fair market value in dollars` is that of ALL its outstanding shares.
-- src:112 | (a) any number of shares that carry 25% or more of the voting rights attached to all of the corporation’s outstanding voting shares; or
-- src:113 | (b) any number of shares that is equal to 25% or more of all of the corporation’s outstanding shares measured by fair market value.
@ref CBCA s 2.1(3) significant number of shares (src:112-113)
DECLARE `Block of shares` HAS
    `votes carried` IS A NUMBER
    `fair market value in dollars` IS A NUMBER

§§ `Time limits -- the one way they are written`

-- The Act's time limits are in days, weeks, months and years ("twenty days", "six months", "two years").
-- A time limit is ONE value: how many units, and which unit. Never a bare number whose unit only a
-- comment knows. `WITHIN n` of a regulative rule counts days; a limit in any other unit is computed to
-- a date with the rules below and compared as dates.
DECLARE `Unit of time` IS ONE OF
    `calendar days`
    `calendar weeks`
    `calendar months`
    `calendar years`

DECLARE `Time limit` HAS
    `number of units` IS A NUMBER
    `unit of time` IS A `Unit of time`

-- "within N units after D": D is the start, and the last day is D plus N units. Months and years use
-- `add months` and `add years`, which clamp to the last day of a shorter month (31 August plus six
-- months is 28 February). The Act's own text does not say how days are counted (the Interpretation Act
-- is not among the sources), so the start day is allowed and the last day is included: this is a
-- convention, recorded as fork F1 in NOTES.md.
GIVEN limit IS A `Time limit`
      start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of` limit `counted from` start MEANS
    CONSIDER limit's `unit of time`
    WHEN `calendar days`   THEN start PLUS (limit's `number of units`)
    WHEN `calendar weeks`  THEN start PLUS ((limit's `number of units`) TIMES 7)
    WHEN `calendar months` THEN `add months` start (limit's `number of units`)
    WHEN `calendar years`  THEN `add years` start (limit's `number of units`)

-- "not more than N units before D" (a notice period, say): the earliest day, counting back from D.
GIVEN limit IS A `Time limit`
      `the later date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the earliest day allowed by` limit `counted back from` `the later date` MEANS
    CONSIDER limit's `unit of time`
    WHEN `calendar days`   THEN `the later date` MINUS (limit's `number of units`)
    WHEN `calendar weeks`  THEN `the later date` MINUS ((limit's `number of units`) TIMES 7)
    WHEN `calendar months` THEN `add months` `the later date` (0 MINUS (limit's `number of units`))
    WHEN `calendar years`  THEN `add years` `the later date` (0 MINUS (limit's `number of units`))

-- "within the time limit": an act done on `act` is in time if it is not before the start and not after
-- the last day.
GIVEN limit IS A `Time limit`
      start IS A DATE
      act IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`done within the time limit` limit start act MEANS
        act AT LEAST start
    AND act AT MOST (`the last day of` limit `counted from` start)

§§ `Dates`

-- The stable prelude has no YMD. Every piece writes YMD year month day; this is the only definition.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YearMonthDay y m d MEANS Date d m y -- renamed from YMD so it cannot collide with the YMD in newer daydate libraries; see docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md section 5
